[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known, with one fix queued for the parser. The reports merged into it
describe more than one problem, and the parser fix covers only one of them. Each problem now has its own ticket.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} repo="krispy-bacon-bits/MegaDev" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day move': 3 }} />

## The problems

**`parser`** ([issue 1](https://github.com/krispy-bacon-bits/MegaDev/issues/1)). The shared parser reads every
date month first, and every tenant on #4471 writes the day first. Day and month come out swapped. "Our 7 April
reminder went out on 4 July."

**`hint`** ([issue 2](https://github.com/krispy-bacon-bits/MegaDev/issues/2)). The booking-v3 form prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date day first. People type what the hint tells them and the
date comes out swapped. This form already reads day first, so the parser fix does not change it.

**`one-day move`** ([issue 3](https://github.com/krispy-bacon-bits/MegaDev/issues/3)). These dates are one day off,
not swapped. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." That points at time zone
handling. The desk listed these reports in `desk-notes.json`, but the table on #4471 still counts them as `parser`.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471 as fixed when the parser fix ships. Issues 2 and 3 stay open until their own fixes land.
</Callout>

## How the reports were counted

- One id, one report. The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice.
- A report goes where its tenant's locale, booking form and channel put it (`causes.ts`), not where intake tagged
  it. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the tags above show how often it guessed wrong.
- The one-day moves come from the desk's own reading in `desk-notes.json`. They stay counted where the desk files
  them and get a row of their own. When the desk adds a report there, it joins that row on the next build.
- Matching on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) is how all of this ended up in
  one ticket. The same subject line turns up under different problems, so subjects cannot tell them apart.

